WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
- What's up y'all?

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:04.003
I'm here with comedian
Joyelle Johnson, give it up!

00:00:07.148 --> 00:00:09.480
So if you've ever
shopped at a flea market,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.970
you've probably seen
those piles of old letters

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.550
and photographs for sale.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.490
You may have wondered, like,
where do these come from?

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.772
What happened to their
owners? Why are they here?

00:00:17.772 --> 00:00:20.520
Well my next guest has
those same questions.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.900
She's had those and decided
to do something about it.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.600
She's known as The Heirloom Hunter.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.140
She collects family
heirlooms and returns them

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.700
to the next of kin, all free of charge.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.333
Say hi to Chelsey everybody!

00:00:34.456 --> 00:00:36.013
So meet Joyelle.

00:00:36.013 --> 00:00:37.037
- Nice to meet you, gorgeous.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.620
- [Chelsey] Her eyeliner is amazing.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:39.453
- [Kelly] I know!

00:00:39.453 --> 00:00:40.286
- We both got fingernails.

00:00:40.286 --> 00:00:41.253
- Yes, that is true, our nails--

00:00:41.253 --> 00:00:42.086
- I know, but the glitter
I've been watching.

00:00:42.086 --> 00:00:44.500
I know all, both your finger,
I know mine are so basic.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.210
Okay, let's move on.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.682
So wait, this is so unique.

00:00:47.682 --> 00:00:49.440
I mean, what's your process like?

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.750
So you buy one, like do
you buy specifically things

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.150
you think you have a chance
at finding? Obviously.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.950
- So I always try and find
items with identifying marks.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:57.936
Names, date, locations.

00:00:57.936 --> 00:00:58.769
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:00.050
- The more the merrier.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:02.710
There is a myth that these items

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:03.930
just end up at the flea market

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.101
because people throw them
out, which is so not the case.

00:01:07.101 --> 00:01:09.400
Most of these items end
up at the flea market

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.680
because of family drama.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.040
So for example, someone
gets access to an estate

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.630
and then doesn't give the items
to the rest of the family.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.520
But basically what I do is I just,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.370
I put in all names and locations
into an online database

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.550
and I just see what pops up.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.900
So sometimes a census record will pop up,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.330
sometimes a public family tree

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.317
where I can message the owner
of that public family tree.

00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:32.680
But it really just depends on each item.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.430
But one of my favorite returns

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:39.430
was actually Valentine's day
cards from around World War II.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.870
And what's special about these cards,

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.040
and what was emotional for me about them

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.620
were honestly, it was just the return.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.650
I returned them to the granddaughter

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.780
of the couple who sent back
and forth Valentine's day cards

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.650
while the husband was in the Navy.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.960
And the granddaughter said to me

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.120
when she received the cards, was,

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.490
it felt like you brought
my grandparents back

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.100
for those few moments that
I was reading the card.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.066
- Oh my God, she's.

00:02:04.066 --> 00:02:05.845
- Girl, I got lashes on.

00:02:05.845 --> 00:02:06.885
- I'm sorry.

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:08.003
- Girl, I got.

00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:09.376
- [Kelly] It's so sweet.

00:02:09.376 --> 00:02:13.180
- But it was one of my
most special returns

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:14.533
and I was weeping, she was weeping,

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:15.950
it was just a lot of weeping.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:16.783
Yeah.

00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:18.125
- She is weeping.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:20.160
Do you have anything in like your,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.820
the only thing I have in
my family thing, I think,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.190
for my mom is my grandmother,
and it's like vinyl records.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.750
And I think my mom gave
them to me, obviously,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:27.870
the musician in the family.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.390
But do you have anything
in your family that you?

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.740
- I have this Swarovski ring

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.691
that I got for my 40th
birthday for myself.

00:02:33.691 --> 00:02:34.524
So...

00:02:36.419 --> 00:02:37.252
this will be a family heirloom.

00:02:37.252 --> 00:02:38.751
- So this will be a family heirloom?

00:02:38.751 --> 00:02:41.170
- Yes. I love it. That
is a family heirloom.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.440
- It will not end up in a thrift store.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.440
- So, we will be passing this on.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.012
You are starting it.

00:02:47.012 --> 00:02:47.845
- Yeah.

00:02:47.845 --> 00:02:48.760
- So wait, so this is beautiful work

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.755
but it's gotta be pretty emotional, right?

00:02:50.755 --> 00:02:51.588
- Yes.

00:02:51.588 --> 00:02:53.060
- Have you ever showed
up and given something

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.198
to someone and then it didn't go well?

00:02:55.198 --> 00:02:58.210
- Yes. So, but not in the
way that you would expect.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.277
So I found love letters from the 1960s

00:03:00.277 --> 00:03:05.184
and I returned them to a
woman who's still alive.

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:06.375
The would-be recipient.

00:03:06.375 --> 00:03:08.990
And she had never received the letters.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.210
So when I gave them to her,

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.517
it was her first time reading them.

00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:13.760
And it was very traumatic for her

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.080
because that was someone
who she was dating

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.300
and she never received those.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.394
She thought he just stopped
corresponding with her,

00:03:20.394 --> 00:03:21.340
but he didn't.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.173
- Oh no!

00:03:22.173 --> 00:03:23.006
- She thought he ghosted?

00:03:23.006 --> 00:03:23.839
- Yeah.

00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:25.580
- Oh my goodness.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.452
- So, it, yeah, yep.

00:03:27.452 --> 00:03:29.221
- It's a first ghosting right there.

00:03:29.221 --> 00:03:30.163
- Yes!

00:03:30.163 --> 00:03:32.050
- And so it brings up that question, like,

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.630
could they have ended up together

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.525
if she did receive the
letters back in the 60s?

00:03:36.525 --> 00:03:39.240
So that was painful for her,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.010
it was painful for me,
but it wasn't painful.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.859
She was so glad that I
did return those letters.

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:43.890
- Yeah.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.870
- But it was more of, or give them to her

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.120
for the first time.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:47.953
But--

00:03:47.953 --> 00:03:49.268
- Because imagine the healing
that could come from that,

00:03:49.268 --> 00:03:50.420
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.912
Like knowing that you weren't ghosted.

00:03:51.912 --> 00:03:52.745
- Yeah.

00:03:52.745 --> 00:03:54.420
- Right? And I think
that did cause, I mean,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.122
listen, it provided closure.

00:03:56.122 --> 00:03:57.000
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:57.833
- For her.

00:03:57.833 --> 00:03:58.666
- [Kelly] Amen.

00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:01.290
- And I mean, she's remarried
now and very happy, but--

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.670
- Wouldn't it be funny if she walked in,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.474
and she was like I could
have ended up with him!

00:04:04.474 --> 00:04:05.360
- Yeah. Wait.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.492
I'm waiting for her to
just walk in right now.

00:04:07.492 --> 00:04:08.325
- Yeah yeah.

00:04:08.325 --> 00:04:09.367
- [Chelsey] Right?

00:04:09.367 --> 00:04:10.210
- So, in some ways

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.550
you're following in
your father's footsteps.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.787
Because I heard he's
a genealogist, was he?

00:04:12.787 --> 00:04:13.970
- [Chelsey] Yes. That is correct.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:14.803
- That's amazing.

00:04:14.803 --> 00:04:16.230
- So my dad is a
genealogist and growing up,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.528
so I'd see him help families
all around the world.

00:04:18.528 --> 00:04:19.361
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:19.361 --> 00:04:20.279
- For free, by the way.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:21.112
And--

00:04:21.112 --> 00:04:22.249
- Is that Downton Abbey?

00:04:22.249 --> 00:04:23.341
- Is that Downton Abbey?

00:04:23.341 --> 00:04:24.783
- That is Downton Abbey.

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:27.570
Yep.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:28.690
- [Joyelle] Your girl knows a castle.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:29.800
- Yep.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.679
I know Downton Abbey very well.

00:04:31.679 --> 00:04:35.860
But I saw him help families
all around the world for free.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.955
And I have to admit growing
up I did not find it cool.

00:04:39.955 --> 00:04:41.187
Especially when I was a teenager.

00:04:41.187 --> 00:04:42.020
- Yeah.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.330
No, we never find cool stuff
cool when we're young, yeah.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.163
- Of course.

00:04:44.163 --> 00:04:46.386
And I do think though it
did hurt my dad's feelings

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:49.142
in a way, but as I grew up

00:04:49.142 --> 00:04:52.240
I started to become more
attached to genealogy.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.583
I started enjoying it more.

00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:54.500
- Yeah.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:55.880
- It's really fantastic.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.848
- So one of your most beloved
discoveries was letters.

00:04:58.848 --> 00:05:01.710
They were letters written
by Holocaust survivor?

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.590
- Survivor. Yes they were. So--

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.930
- How intense was that, first
of all? Just reading it.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.340
- I was, well, since starting this journey

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.230
I have to admit that
Holocaust documentation

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.770
has been my number one priority,

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:12.910
getting it off of the market.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.012
Unfortunately, people do
sell Holocaust documentation

00:05:15.012 --> 00:05:18.708
either underground or at
auction for very high prices.

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:22.300
But reading that letter
was very emotional for me,

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.120
especially that return.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.548
The letter was basically to say,

00:05:25.548 --> 00:05:30.548
it was the day that Ilse
left the camp and she,

00:05:31.382 --> 00:05:33.600
the letter was to her sister in England

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.960
basically telling her that
everyone in her family had died.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.410
Mom, dad, two sisters and her husband.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:45.610
And so yes, but the
story behind this letter

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:48.280
and how I got it is
actually pretty interesting.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.100
There's a Spender that I
always use in New York city.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.823
Well, not anymore, we'll get to that.

00:05:52.823 --> 00:05:56.470
But I frequently get
love letters from him.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.790
And I remember in passing in October

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.217
he mentioned that he had
Holocaust documentation at home,

00:06:02.217 --> 00:06:04.510
hundreds of documents
that he was gonna sell

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.650
for thousands of dollars. Disgusting.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:09.134
But I knew I had to get
my hands on those items.

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:13.621
So I started this strategy
to get him, to persuade him

00:06:13.621 --> 00:06:16.920
to show me the letters, to bring them in.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:17.753
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:17.753 --> 00:06:20.750
- And so I found the
translation of that letter

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:24.381
and the second that I
found the translation

00:06:24.381 --> 00:06:25.910
and I knew what it was,

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.050
I knew I couldn't tell
him what it actually was

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.130
because if I did, he either
wouldn't have sold me the letter

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.330
or would've sold it to me
for thousands of dollars.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.750
So I told a little fib and I just said,

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.060
the letter was about food stamps

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.830
and could I buy it to give to the family?

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.210
And so I did. I bought it, but.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.043
- Sneaky!

00:06:44.043 --> 00:06:46.020
- Sneaky, but, and to be honest,

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.282
I would do it again in a heartbeat.

00:06:47.282 --> 00:06:48.115
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:48.115 --> 00:06:48.948
- And I was able to--

00:06:48.948 --> 00:06:50.124
- You're trying to get it to the family.

00:06:50.124 --> 00:06:50.957
- [Chelsey] Yes.

00:06:50.957 --> 00:06:51.790
- Yeah.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:52.834
- [Chelsey] And there's a moral
aspect that comes with it.

00:06:52.834 --> 00:06:53.667
- I mean sneaky in a good way.

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:54.890
There's good sneaky.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.640
- I would do it again in
a heartbeat if I could.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.060
- Oh, well we actually have
Ilse's great niece Jill

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.570
dialed in right now.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:02.523
So, hi Jill.

00:07:04.302 --> 00:07:05.700
- Hi.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:06.580
- So Jill,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.160
what did you think when you
first heard from Chelsey?

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.346
- I was really blown away
and amazed by her kindness

00:07:13.346 --> 00:07:16.700
and the fact that she would
spend her time and energies

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:21.700
reaching out to connect me with a letter

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.740
in the actual handwriting
of my favorite great aunt

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.020
who was really more like a
grandmother to me growing up

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.420
because she didn't have
any children of her own.

00:07:31.148 --> 00:07:33.660
And she, herself in her youth,

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.810
they say she looked like the
actress, Marlene Dietrich.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:39.950
She was blonde and blue eyed,
so she could pass as Aryan.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:42.270
She actually was part of
an underground organization

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:45.660
in Berlin that was actually
protesting the fascists

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.620
early into the 1940s.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:49.920
And then when she was captured

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.680
she was actually sent on a
transport train to Auschwitz,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.540
which amazingly she jumped off.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.620
One of the only people who
survived such transports

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.060
and went back to Berlin,
was hidden underground.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:03.410
And this letter is
shortly after liberation.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:05.584
She's writing to her
only surviving sister.

00:08:05.584 --> 00:08:08.520
And as Chelsey said, she's telling them

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.460
for the first time that
their other two sisters,

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.240
their brother-in-law, their parents,

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.068
and Ilse's second husband Gerhardt

00:08:17.068 --> 00:08:19.880
had all been murdered.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.530
And it's just unbelievable
that this is the emotion

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:25.823
that she must have had
to put in this letter,

00:08:27.399 --> 00:08:28.400
to send it.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.379
And on the receiving end,
to be the one to find out

00:08:31.379 --> 00:08:33.745
that your family is gone.

00:08:33.745 --> 00:08:36.844
- Yeah, I don't get.
I, can you imagine like

00:08:36.844 --> 00:08:39.760
unexpectedly just
receiving family letters?

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:40.954
Like anything like that?

00:08:40.954 --> 00:08:41.830
- Oh, yeah.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:44.050
Well, my great great grandfather

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:45.610
was a part of the slave narrative.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.180
So we like, he sent, you
know, letters and stuff,

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.320
and they documented his
experience in slavery. So.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.360
- So you have actually?

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.193
- [Joyelle] Yeah.

00:08:53.193 --> 00:08:54.080
- That's hard.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:55.391
- Yeah. Absolutely.

00:08:55.391 --> 00:08:58.240
- But also a reason to like, I guess,

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.232
be so thankful for that,

00:08:59.232 --> 00:09:02.010
for the fact that like you
get this life right now,

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:02.843
you know what I'm saying?

00:09:02.843 --> 00:09:04.129
Like we all get this life right now.

00:09:04.129 --> 00:09:05.130
- Absolutely. Yes.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.675
- Jill, what does it mean
to you and your family

00:09:07.675 --> 00:09:09.667
to have these letters back?

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:13.000
And you know, it's in in her
words, it's in her handwriting.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.666
That's pretty amazing.

00:09:15.666 --> 00:09:17.500
- It's really wonderful.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.831
And the letters themselves,
like the one that you showed,

00:09:20.831 --> 00:09:23.180
the first line, it translated says

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.380
through the kindness of our liberators.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:28.607
So that really epitomized my great aunt,

00:09:28.607 --> 00:09:32.007
of what she was like, that
just, this sense of gratitude

00:09:32.007 --> 00:09:34.040
she kept up after the war,

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.010
when the tables turned
and she was in the US,

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:39.310
she sent needed supplies and money

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.950
to the people who had hidden her

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:47.400
and helped them rebuild their
lives in postwar Germany.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.920
And just to have this
memorial that I hope to frame

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:53.160
and put in my room in my home,

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.783
so that anybody who sees them
can see and ask me about it,

00:09:56.783 --> 00:09:58.950
can learn Chelsey's story,

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:01.050
and learn that there are people
who do good in the world.

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:04.560
That Chelsey is someone who
takes their own energy and time

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.490
and money and efforts and talents

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.524
and reaches out and
connects people like me

00:10:10.524 --> 00:10:12.842
with a piece of my family that's gone

00:10:12.842 --> 00:10:15.698
is, I'm forever grateful

00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:18.940
- And history is so
important. So important.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.440
So we don't forget it.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.330
- No. We can't forget. Never forget.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:24.693
That's why people say never forget.

00:10:24.693 --> 00:10:26.990
- I love that. Thank you so much, Chelsey.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:30.307
Thank you so much, Jill, for sharing.

